`wexample/symfony-analytics-ds` draws symfony-analytics for its administrator.

## The audience page

`/{_locale}/analytics/` (`analytics_index`), for symfony-analytics' `access_role`, from top to bottom:

- an audience-nav (`analytics-audience-nav`: people, robots, everything) and a period-nav (month, year, or the last 7, 30, 90 days), both kept in the address;
- the figures of the audience chosen (`analytics-totals`);
- who reads the site: every page read per day, stacked per audience — the raw traffic, whatever the audience chosen;
- the people's visitors, visits and pages over time, and their world map;
- the tables of the audience chosen: countries, then the origins — sources, engines and assistants, sites, keywords, campaigns, devices —, the pages by route then by address (each a link);
- the robots, declared and suspected, whatever the audience chosen;
- what the audience weighs in the database and how long each audience is kept day by day.

Each table is an `analytics-breakdown-table` (an entity table on `AnalyticsBreakdown`), given its `audience` or following the audience-nav.

Every part follows the period-nav: it listens to `period-change`, asks for the period once listening (`period-request`), and reads the api again at each change. The audience-nav is followed the same way (`analytics-audience-change`, `analytics-audience-request`). The map credits DB-IP, as its licence asks.
